      "curve_hash": "sha256:b5dd65073f162cd3427be4668de3a4f7ec32547a48a9515b7bfc7f24a609888d",
      "shock": "An exfiltration of the account and transaction record store suppresses fee income for a quarter.\nTHE TRIPLE IS AN ANCHOR, NOT A MEASUREMENT, and it is graded 3 for exactly that reason. This institution has no incident of its own to measure, so the three numbers are arithmetic on a comparable UK challenger bank's own published regulatory record, nested from the narrowest reading to the widest:\n\n  min  0.0640 = 28,959,426 / 452,800,000\n  mode 0.0698 = 28,959,426 / 414,814,000\n  max  0.0987 = 40,959,426 / 414,814,000\n\n28,959,426 GBP is the financial penalty in the FCA's Final Notice against Starling Bank Limited of 2024-09-27 (reference 730166), and 40,959,426 GBP is the same notice's figure before the 30% stage-one settlement discount. 452,800,000 GBP is the revenue the same notice states at 2.1 for 2023; 414,814,000 GBP is Total Income for the year ended 2023-03-31 in that bank's own audited Annual Report 2023, a different metric on a different reporting convention. Sources and the discrepancy are recorded in the hub's .scratch/ecosystem/research/94-studied-firms.md.\nWhat it is NOT: a conduct penalty is not a suppression of fee income, and that firm is not this one. Grade 3 on the published ladder is \"an established mechanism from published work, not observed here\", which is precisely the claim being made. The institution's party artefact declares pricing_threshold: 3, so this unchanged grade is admissible at that declared threshold. The cash flow remains derived from its recorded native GBP filing fact; no comparable penalty becomes this institution's incident or a measurement of suppressed fee income. A grade-1 or grade-2 replacement would require this institution's own dated incident record, and a regrade event must record who moved it and why (twin/evidence.py).",
